<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cædmon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 657–680</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cædmon was a cowherd at the monastery of Whitby under Abbess Hild who, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, received the gift of composing religious verse in a dream. His nine-line hymn on the Creation, preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Bede's Ecclesiastical History, is among the earliest recorded poems in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a>. He became a monk and turned scripture into Old English verse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caedmon.jpg" alt="Cædmon" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rich Tea · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MemorialToCaedmon(RichardThomas)Jul2006.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
